11.0.0 — clip edges in Arrange, a quiet click, safer listening and export, and a studio that says what it does

New since 10081, built by the Echoel Studio run; nothing here is device-verified yet.
- Arrange: a note or drum clip has a grip at its start and its end (on a block of at least 88 pt).
  Drag the end to shorten or lengthen it; drag the start and every note stays where it sounds. The
  edge follows the magnet, stops at the next clip and at bar 1. One Undo ("Trim Clip").
- Click: off at every launch. Its level is in the More menu and on the Click key's hold menu,
  −36 to −6 dB, starting at −24 dB; the count-in plays at the same level.
- Listening in the browser is mixed before the output's guard, so it cannot pass full scale.
- Video export: footage that flashes is drawn darker in the film, as the video window shows it
  (three flashes a second at most). The export reads the footage twice, so it takes longer.
- A refused permission (microphone, camera, Local Network, speech, camera pulse) has Open Settings.
- A refused edit in Arrange and the track list says why in the status line instead of snapping back.
- Piano roll: a new install opens at rows of a full touch target (45 pt); a note's length edge is 22 pt.
- Song menu, Connect: Light Show… opens fixtures and cues directly.
- Arrange: "Remove from Song" takes a clip or region out of one place; "Delete Clip Everywhere"
  deletes the clip (also in Session).
- A clipping meter shows a light cap and VoiceOver says "clipped"; an empty Mixer and Session say
  where to start; names shrink before they are cut at large text; Increase Contrast strengthens the
  hairlines; Escape closes a sheet on an iPad keyboard; VoiceOver reaches a track's menu in Arrange
  and hears the bar of views as tabs.
Limits, new: a block narrower than 88 pt shows no grips (trim it in the editor's length menu, or
zoom in); the app does not read Bold Text yet.
Older limits as in 10081.

Previous: 10081. A green commit that changes this file uploads build 10000 + run number. A test
list, not a source for claims (ContentPipeline/CLAIMS.md).

One ask, as for 10081. Start a screen recording, open the app, and try to make sixteen bars in two
to five minutes: a beat, a bass line or a chord, then Play. No list and no protocol; stop wherever
it stops being possible or pleasant. The recording is the answer. What gets in the way in it is
the next work, in the order it happened.

The five checks of 10079 stay open and unchanged and rest until that recording exists.
